## Decisions

### Who should be allowed to attach email addresses to an existing outside guest?

I confirmed an identity takeover through the shared write API. `PERSON_EMAIL` stores the addresses used to identify incoming mail senders; `PERSON` stores each member or outside guest. Today, any signed-in member who can bring guests can add an address to any existing guest, without managing that guest's lists. An attacker can attach a mailbox they control and send authenticated mail with that guest's posting permissions.

The regression test uses the sample outside guest, gives them `guest@example.net`, and puts them on a private members-only list. Rowan Ashdown then attaches `intruder@example.net` to that guest through `/api/q`. The server returns HTTP 200; the new address identifies the guest and can post to the private list. No production code has changed.

The audit instructions explicitly require your decision when a fix determines who is allowed to act. I recommend assigning guest addresses only during creation: the documented invitation and Loop flows already create a new guest and their email together.

- (*) Assign addresses only at guest creation
  Before: members could add addresses to an established guest.
  Now: ordinary writes must create the guest and their address in the same batch; existing guests require a super admin to change their addresses.
  On the reproduced input, Rowan's addition of `intruder@example.net` is refused, even if Rowan hosts one of the guest's events. Creating a separate new guest with that address still succeeds. This also means members cannot later supply an address for a guest originally created without one.
- ( ) Allow hosts with authority over every affected group
  Before: members could add addresses to an established guest without hosting any affected group.
  Now: a member may add an address only when they manage every group containing that guest; super admins retain access.
  On the reproduced input, Rowan's addition is refused while Rowan does not manage the private list. If Rowan becomes the host of every group containing that guest, the same addition succeeds and mail from `intruder@example.net` acts as the guest. This preserves later address entry but needs broader authorization checks across memberships.

